  "query_preview": "..Some high clouds will stream across the area today as \na long-wave trough slowly forms over the eastern Pacific. This will \ncontinue into Thursday, although hghts will not change that much. \nThis means 2 more days of relatively mild and dry weather, with \nmainly light southwesterly winds each afternoon.\n\nThis developing long-wave trough will become the focal point for our \nweather from Friday onward, with 2 distinct weather producers on \nFriday, and again on Sunday.\n\nThe first wave of rainfall begins very early Friday, likely peaking \nover the south-central deserts Friday afternoon. This is in response \nto amplifying southwesterly flow that will pick up remnant tropical \nmoisture from the Gulf of California and northern Sonora. Some of \nthis moisture is from the decaying T.S. Vance and some is still our \nremnant Monsoonal moisture pool, that has never really been swept \nout. A weak s/wv trough in the southwesterlies will act on this \nmoisture, bringing about a 12 hour period of precipitation. It does \nlook as though most of this will be showery, possibly tied closely \nto upslope regions. The risk of strong thunderstorms is low at this \ntime.\n\nThis moisture is shunted east Friday night and into Saturday, with \nweak subsidence behind the exiting s/wv. Moist southwesterly flow \npersists SaTurday, so some drippy showers remain possible, but not \nas widespread as Friday.\n\nLater Saturday night and into Sunday, a much more significant event \nis possible as the main long-wave trough moves inland. As it does \nso, it will attempt to merge with a sub-tropical wave moving through \nthe eastern Pacific at around 28N/29N. This may be a potent scenario \nand could lead to an extended period of moderate to locally heavy \nrainfall. Additionally, increased shear, large-scale ascent and \ncolder air aloft raises the risk for severe weather. Due to the \nuncertainty with these 2 waves ability to phase, it is better to \ntake a partially conservative approach. This still results in fairly \nwidespread and rainy conditions, but a bit early to jump on board \nwith severe weather and extensive flooding.\n\nIt is important to mentally prepare for the possibility of severe \nweather and extensive flooding, but that is not quite the official \nforecast yet. The combination of the 2 events could produce 1 to 4 \ninches of rain across the area, especially over the south-central \ndeserts and just south of the Mogollon Rim. Stay tuned for updates \nin the coming days.",
